From ba27309734549795e03e1b70bf90f7f2bb80975c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Murka-Moncz Zsigmond <sanyikapenz@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 09:29:54 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Use hassh64 for texture cache invalidation hashes

MaskedHash() hashes every texture and palette range when a cache entry
is created and again whenever the VRAM range it covers is marked dirty.
Switch it from XXH64 to hassh64 (MIT, single header, vendored as
src/hassh/hassh.h with its license notice). Wrapped ranges are still
chained by passing the previous hash as the seed, the hashes only live
in memory, and the function signature is unchanged.

On x86-64 without -maes the header picks its AES-NI path at run time;
other targets get a portable C path with identical output.

MaskedHash on random VRAM contents, best of 7, one core (Core Ultra 7
265KF), Release flags (-O3, no -march):

  bytes     XXH64      hassh64    XXH3 (seeded, for reference)
  8         2.3 ns     2.6 ns     1.7 ns
  32        5.1 ns     3.5 ns     2.2 ns
  512       24.3 ns    14.6 ns    12.5 ns
  2 KiB     85.8 ns    42.7 ns    44.9 ns
  8 KiB     322 ns     150 ns     172 ns
  32 KiB    1.27 us    0.55 us    0.68 us
  128 KiB   5.06 us    2.15 us    2.71 us

That is about 2.3x XXH64 for texture-sized ranges (61 vs 26 GB/s at
128 KiB). With -march=native (VAES/AVX2) hassh64 reaches 118 GB/s at
128 KiB, XXH3 79 GB/s, XXH64 unchanged. Palette-sized ranges up to
32 bytes are within a nanosecond either way; at 8 bytes hassh64 is
0.3 ns slower than XXH64.
---
 src/GPU3D_Texcache.h | 5 ++---
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/GPU3D_Texcache.h b/src/GPU3D_Texcache.h
index 7e8a95d..f59ad39 100644
--- a/src/GPU3D_Texcache.h
+++ b/src/GPU3D_Texcache.h
@@ -8,8 +8,7 @@
 #include <unordered_map>
 #include <vector>
 
-#define XXH_STATIC_LINKING_ONLY
-#include "xxhash/xxhash.h"
+#include "hassh/hassh.h"
 
 namespace melonDS
 {
@@ -62,7 +61,7 @@ public:
                 // fits completely inside
                 pieceSize = size;
 
-            hash = XXH64(&vram[addr], pieceSize, hash);
+            hash = hassh64(&vram[addr], pieceSize, hash);
 
             addr += pieceSize;
             addr &= (vramSize - 1);
-- 
